Z||000005||Nashco||10-15-2001||01:04 AM||||The link works for me...???<P>Archie, I agree with you, for the most part.  When it comes to a market, I like to use cookies for a comparison.  You can buy the fresh store made ones...but that's like buying the expensive stuff for 5% better taste.  Or you can get the real deal...the chips ahoy, E.L. fudge, or oreos.  These taste great, and lots of research has gone into them to make them the best.  They advertise a fair amount, and as a result of these added costs, their cookie costs a little more.  For some, it's worth it (myself included...I'm a sucker for chips ahoy <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  Then there's the super cheap store brand.  Quality is usually not there, and the flair just isn't there.  They can try to copy, but you're never gonna get the same thing.  As a result, the price is a lot lower, but they're never the real deal.  Lastly, there's home made, which is almost always the best, but at the cost of doing all the work yourself.<P>Now, I agree, some people have taken some blatant copies.  Then again, a copy isn't the real thing, and usually of lesser quality in dimensions or slight differences.  As copies of copies are made down the line, quality drops exponentially, in my opinion.  Remember Outlaw, the recent copycat of some fiero body parts?  They were a copycat, and almost instantly grew a reputation for selling junk.  I, along with many others, am not willing to buy poor quality or "close enough to good" parts.  I am a soon to be entrepeneur myself, and would be very upset if somebody ripped my design...but I would not be afraid of the competition.  I know I am just another step up on them because my parts are of better quality, and I'm ahead of them in the next design update.  It's like how intel always seems to be a step ahead of amd in the processor world, because they can release the next version just a hair sooner.<P>In conclusion, and to explain my feelings, I am not totally against free enterprise.  I'd even go as far to say that if you buy a part, make a copy of it, and then add some further improvements and modifications, I'd accept that.  This sort of practice "doesn't seem fair" but it does cause a lot of competition driving towards higher quality parts and better buyer options.  I know it would be nice to dominate a market because you were there first, but that's not how things work.  If you want it like that, make a patent; still, you will only have full rights to a design for so long before you will lose the ability to have the only one out there.  If somebody is taking a direct copy of a part, as TFS did recently, that is unacceptable.  I am completely against this sort of practice, unless it is to build parts/a part that is no longer produced.<P>As a side note, I think that IRM would get a lot more business not by dropping prices, but if they had a quicker return on part orders.  It seems to me I've heard an awful lot of people complain, or mention, that their orders took anywhere from a month to nearly a year.  That sort of information definitely doesn't put a warm fuzzy feeling in my about that company; sometimes it seems I'd almost be better off buying the poor quality parts, and fixing them myself.  If I had a deadline to meet, I'm pretty sure this is my only option...???<P>Archie, you are not the only "original" one who I support in your endeavours as an entrepeneur, so please don't think I'm against you with this.  I'm just trying to bring another point of view to the issue; I'm sure you know some of these points, and perhaps discount most, if not all, of them for one reason or another.  I guess you could consider this a buyer's standpoint, be it just or not from a builder's/seller's standpoint, such as yours.<P>I would love to hear what others think about this, as it seems to have been causing a lot of muddy waters in the fiero circle lately.  Muddy or not, I still like this pond a lot more than any other one I've been a part of <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pontiaddict||10-15-2001||01:35 AM||||If you're gonna make an exact copy of a part, work out a deal with the original producer of the part. If you make a distinguishably (if that's even a word) different part from someone elses work. You can at least argue that it's your own creation, possibly based on their design, but still your own.||||reg||1
