Z||000010||sloth85GT||10-15-2001||05:16 AM||||If Pat can make them cheaper than IRM and still make a profit on an equal quality product, then that means IRM is ripping people off. That's why I'd rather buy from Pat. I'm not made of money, and many other guys here are not. But if someone makes a product and sells it for an obsene amount of money, they're making a profit too big, and that's unfair to us, the consumers.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">     <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||10-15-2001||05:41 AM||||I have nothing against IRM.  They have some truely cool products.  But, here's my take on this.  IRM should have had their R&D costs paid for a long time ago.  They've had some of thier parts on the market for an awfully long time.  IRM may have good quality parts, but from what I understand, their service sucks.  I don't know why and I couldn't care less from a customer standpoint.  (If the owner's sick, I wish him the best, but it still takes forever to get the parts.)  From what I've heard, Pat's products are of a good quality.  If he can produce a part that nobody can get anywhere else (as it seems with most of IRMs parts), he should have the right irregardless of price.  <P>Let me use you for an example, Archie.  You sell V8 conversion kits as a living.  If someone wants to buy a kit from you and you stall them for what could be months, you'll lose business.  If I decide that I can sell the same thing and get it out to them right away, I'll take your business.  And I won't feel bad about it one bit.  On the other hand, if you continue your great service and I try to do the same thing, then it's theft on my part.<P>Rodney's situation was different than this IRM/Pat Kalsmith thing.  Rodney's still selling his parts and (I would assume) gets them out in a timely manner (at least he's sent me the parts I've ordered right away).<P>Bottom line for me is this.  I've wanted IRMs Integrated Rocker Panels just about forever.  I haven't purchased them because I've just never really had $550 to spare and even if I did, I don't want to wait what could be a year for the parts.  If Pat decides to make/sell a copy of the IRPs for a decent price with good quality and provides good service, I'll buy from him.  If he doesn't, I won't.  If IRM decides to drop their price and get the parts out in a timely manner, I'll buy from them 1<sup>st</sup> only because they're the originators.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Phil||10-15-2001||07:07 AM||||All of the replies to Archies post go to strengthen his original assertion. BTW TFS was going to sell their bracketry for less than Rodney.<BR>This is my one and only post to this thread||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||10-15-2001||07:54 AM||||kinda mixed feelings. i have the irm panels and it was a kinda frustrating wait, but i never got the feeling i wouldn't recieve them eventualy, plus they were made pretty well. when i wanted another set i went with outlaw not because of time or quality issues with irm. i did it just because of price. if you've been around you know where that got me.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>even now seeing these denny patton ones i'm wondering about them instead of trying for another irm set (partialy because of how they're made too. my car has no ribbing to attach the top part of an irm set to. i'd have to try and alter an irm set myself). if pat or someone else makes a set and cuts the price substantially, i can't help but want to look into it. <P>i guess as an end user/consumer it's hard to just say i'm going to spend a couple hundred dollars more for something when  i see it cheaper from someone else. i think if you bought from irm you'll be happy with what you get, but you'll still wonder if you could have done better on the price. <P>maybe irm should turn the tables and make a cheaper version (like the denny patton/outlaw type that don't come all the way up to the ribbing trim in front and use the stock ribbing in back), if that didn't seem like competing with themselves.    ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Archie||10-15-2001||07:59 AM||||BTW, I understand that Jim Sutter does in fact have Patents on his major original items (rockers included). <P>Most likely he hasn't moved on this subject because he too is waiting for pictures to appear. <P>I see some very interesting arguements above from people trying to make the case that this is different from the TFS situation.<P>This is exactly the same situation as the TFS deal. Except that IRM is not represented on this Forum. So I guess that makes it ok to copy their products.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000015||Sage||10-15-2001||08:04 AM||||Generally, I stay away from stuff like this, but I feel compelled to inject some of my personal insights here.<P>I don't know Pat, but he seems to me, from the impression left here on the forum, to be a talented, conscientious, hard working, experienced craftsman.  Seems like he wants to serve the Fiero community, and make a living, without trying to do it from 3 sales.  In other words, he produces desirable products at an affordable price.  That in itself is commendable, in my opinion.<P>Having just entered the "market place" with my version of full quarter panel sidescoops,I have had to deal with the issues Archie pointed out.  Working with fiberglass, producing parts, is time consuming, dirty, messy, sticky, itchy, smelly hard work.  Doing a new design, building a prototype,(plug), is one of the hardest and most time consuming steps.  Done properly, the plug has to be "perfect", as any imperfections in the plug, will obviously be transferred to the mold, which in turn will be transferred to each part that comes out of it.  Absolute perfection does not exist in this plane, so the goal is "as close to perfect as humanly possible".  That takes many, many hours of plain grunt work, nitpicking yourself to death, and reworking things till you "get it right".  <P>Using someone else's finished product as your plug, is a huge savings in time, energy and effort, not to mention expense,(haven't found anybody yet that will "donate" materials).  Even doing that, there is much work to do, as wherever your plug comes from, whether you build it or buy it, it has to have a mirror like, perfect finish on it in order to make a mold from it.  <P>Then we come to mold building.  This is another step that stops allot of folks, even if they have the knowledge and experience to work with fiberglass, proper mold making is a whole different animal. For a "good" mold, the materials cost more than for parts production, and involve different techniques and procedures.  If anything major goes wrong during the process, you may be faced with chucking the whole thing, plug and all, and starting over.  Fortunately. that does not happen very often, but it has happened.  Then you are talking about throwing away countless hours of your time, and hundreds, even thousands of dollars,(if it is a large mold"). <P>So there are definite advantages to using an existing part to start your project.  However; I would not even consider doing this unless I was planning on making MAJOR changes, in which case, you would be just as ahead of the game to use a panel off the car, or use foam, or clay, or wood, or any material that works to build a form.  When making the plugs for my scoops, I used panels from the car to make the plugs.  I don't consider that "copying" as it was much faster, easier and cheaper than building the whole panel from scratch.  Is this an infringement on GM?  Not in my opinion, as the stock panels have the correct shape, size and fit.  A major modification was made making them a different product than the stock panels.<P>I was offered a set of IRM panels and asked if I wanted to make molds from them.  I said no, I make my own plugs.  That is just me.  I have a set of IRM panels now that are to be put on a customers car.  They are not, in my opinion, what is considered good fiberglass parts.  Don't know if they were made in a hurry, if the molds are breaking down, or if maybe they were training a new layup man, or what, but they are pretty bad as glass goes.  If Pat can produce a quality part by using a set of the originals, especially if IRM is not producing them any longer, He should probably go for it, but I would still suggest starting from scratch and just mimicking the design.   <P>I could drag this out further and tell you about other offers to make molds from other people's parts, but it is too long winded as it is.  Bottom line is I won't do it, and I don't think anybody else should either.<P>As Archie has pointed out, nobody knows the fine points like the guy that did all the work.  Making molds from someone else's parts is like a third grader putting his name on somebody else's paper IMHO.<P>No critisism intended here, just stating my opinion.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||Fiero5||10-15-2001||10:14 AM||||I used to work for an electronics company that would do reverse engineering. This is the process of obtaining the competitors part, and then taking it apart and documenting how it was made. However, the company I worked for did not then take the part and do an "exact" copy. They would make improvements, add updated parts, make it smaller or more efficiant. In doing this they would be making a different though similar part.<P>I agree with Archie that this is no different than Rodney's situation. I am all for Pat making these rocker penels. I will be one of the first in line to obtain a set or two, however I will not even consider it if it is at the expense of another like the Rodney situation. Pat has to do something different like add ridges, or a slightly different shaped opening, have screening built in the opening or something, and not do an exact reproduction. Here is a great oportunity to take a cool product and improve on it or make it different.<P>Having said that, is it possible that we are jumping the gun here? Is Pat planning on making an "exact" repro? Has he said that specifically? If so, then it was a good idea on Archies part to get this out in the open now, before Pat spends time and money on something he might have to then change or scrap.<P>Steve||||reg||1
Z||000017||Steve Normington||10-15-2001||12:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>I see some very interesting arguements above from people trying to make the case that this is different from the TFS situation.<P>This is exactly the same situation as the TFS deal. Except that IRM is not represented on this Forum. So I guess that makes it ok to copy their products.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Or, as I posted here, "big company bad, small company (or individual) good". TFS is a big company and Rodney is an individual, therefore Rodney is right, and TFS is wrong. IRM is a big company and Pat is an individual, therefore IRM is wrong and Pat is right. Sorry for the double rant, but this is a pet peeve of mine. [/rant]<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||10-15-2001||12:28 PM||||It's called a free-market. Where "honor" kicks in is arbitrary. In business, "nice" means going under.<P>Odds are most of us work for companies that do exactly what Archie and Rodney complain about. It just seems worse because we kinda' know these people.<P>The consumer drives the market. Buy from those you want to survive. But regardless of what we think, it's legal and frankly if someone could make millions, they'd likely copy other people's stuff too.<P>Either patent it or have it stolen. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Fiero_24||10-15-2001||12:58 PM||||Archie, does this have anything to do with the rumor of you maybe purchasing IRM?  This is what I have heard, and plesae correct me if I am wrong.  What about Ford?  They made the trunk scoop, or should I say hood scoop.  I do not see ford going after the Fiero community for the scoop.  There are always copy's out there, even kit cars that ar copies of copies.  I have bought from IRM and think that they are great, and my parts were shipped quickly.  I do understand your argument Archie, and I would be mad if someone took my hard work and started selling it.  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||5
Z||000020||theogre||10-15-2001||08:52 PM||||To blatantly rip off a design is wrong and may actually be illegal in this case... Body/bodypart designs usually fall under copyright laws from everything I've seen in the last few years. That is the avenue that Lambo and others are using to persue Kit cars. Copyright is a whole diferant set of rules from patent. Usually all you need is to prove yours came first.<P>I'm not going to get into who ripped off who name calling... There aren't enough details for allot of that. As I understand it from this thread, the one guy doesn't even have a hard product yet.<P>Being first with something doesn't make it best worst or whatever... Still doesn't mean you can simply rip it off regardless of how much you like or dislike the companies involved.<P>Since someone mentioned patents.... Before you make remarks about patents... go to to <A HREF="http://www.uspto.gov" TARGET=_blank>www.uspto.gov</A>  and have a look at what getting one costs just in PTO fees. Add that to whatever legal cost you have to spend in that process. Patenting a product is anyhting but cheap. it can take months of legal  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> just to have a proper patent search done and you can't get a patent without it.<P>For info about U.S. copyright, <A HREF="http://www.loc.gov" TARGET=_blank>www.loc.gov</A>  has all that.<P>For info on international aspects of copyright and patent stuff look up WIPO. (World Intelectual Property Org if I remember right.)<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">    <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||Jaygee79||10-15-2001||10:18 PM||||I have no opinion on the subject (actually I do but I'm not here to state that).  I just wanted to say that I find it sort of interesting that there weren't all these "free market" comments in the other post.||||reg||1
Z||000022||ferrari_momo||10-16-2001||10:10 PM||||Dear Archie,<BR> Sorry for not getting back to you sooner.  Thank you for taking your time to write me on this topic.  You are a friend and once a heroe of mine.  I respect your opinion and as well as the rest of the people here.  To make this short and simple.  I will not make those side rocker panels.  Instead, i will make my own design.  The project hasn't started yet.  I was trying to raise money for the project.  I do not want to cause any troubles with other business people.  And I am here for the customers.  Customers are like partners in business, friends, food provider, investers, and so on.<BR>  Not every Fiero owners are rich.  Many are still in college and some are still trying to make it.  I know that feelings, been there.  Fieros are great.  Fiero owners are great too.  The fibersmith will grow one day.  I am hoping to expand to Import car market.  But I will not leave fiero people.  I will be here for you all.  When I thought of this "the fibersmith" name.  i thought of something further.  Perhaps something like: motorcycle parts, boats, carbon fiber products, furnitures, toys, etc.  This is only the beginning.  <BR>  Thank you to all my customers.  The profit helps bringing new developments, buy tools, maintain existing tools, working facility, and etc.  I am not making a lot of sales.  I have full time job.  that is my main source of income.  Any one that has a full time job knows how tired it can be to have another job.  If the fibersmith really was doing that well, i would have quit my day time job and pursue the business full time.  thanks agian for all your inputs.  Hope this will solve the case.  Peace be with us all.  <BR>sincerely yours,<BR>Pat Jon Kalsmith <A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A>   <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
